<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ajanta Caves</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2nd century BCE–5th century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ajanta Caves are about thirty Buddhist rock-cut monasteries and prayer halls in a horseshoe-shaped gorge of the Waghora river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The earliest were cut around the 2nd and 1st centuries BCE; most of the rest date from the late 5th century, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakataka-dynasty">Vakataka</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harishena">Harishena</a> and his nobles. Their wall paintings of the Buddha's lives are the finest surviving ancient Indian paintings, and the site is a World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ajanta-caves.jpg" alt="Ajanta Caves" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Soman · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ajanta_(63).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The caves were made in two phases. The first began around the 2nd century BCE and belongs to the 2nd century BCE to 1st century CE. Older accounts place the second phase between 400 and 650 CE, but later scholarship dates it to a brief period of 460 to 480. Walter Spink places the whole second phase in the 5th century. He holds that the earlier group, dated only approximately to between 100 BCE and 100 CE, was then abandoned for over three centuries. According to him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakataka-dynasty">Vakataka</a> emperor Harishena, who reigned from 460 until his death in 477, sponsored numerous new caves, and work stopped soon after his death, which left many caves unfinished.</p><p>The caves are carved from flood basalt of the Deccan Traps, layered horizontally and of variable quality. Excavation began with a narrow tunnel at roof level, which was widened downwards and outwards, as the unfinished Caves 21 to 24 and Cave 28 show. Flaws in the rock caused cracks and collapses, such as that of the portico of Cave 1.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The complex is carved into a 75-metre wall of rock and consists of monasteries (viharas) and worship halls (chaityas) of different Buddhist traditions. There are 36 identifiable foundations, and the numbering from 1 to 29 is a convenience that does not follow the order of construction. Cave 10 is a vast apsidal prayer hall of about the 1st century BCE, with 39 octagonal pillars and a stupa at the end with a path for walking round it. Textual records suggest that the caves served as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> retreat for monks and as a resting place for merchants and pilgrims.</p><p>Most of the wall paintings come from the second phase and mainly narrate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jataka-tales">Jataka tales</a>, the legends of the Buddha's previous births as an animal or a human, including scenes from Aryasura's Jatakamala. Caves 1, 2, 16 and 17 hold the largest body of surviving ancient Indian wall painting. Spink holds that Cave 1, among the last excavated, was never dedicated for worship, and that its stress on royal imagery reflects <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harishena">Harishena</a>'s patronage. A fresco in Cave 2 shows children at a school.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Medieval Chinese Buddhist travellers mention the caves, which were later covered by jungle. They were brought to contemporary attention in 1819 when Colonel John Smith came upon them during a tiger-hunting expedition. The caves are in the northern wall of the U-shaped gorge of the Waghur river, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-plateau">Deccan Plateau</a>, and form a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. Ellora, about 100 kilometres away, has caves of a similar period, and the Ajanta style appears there and at other cave sites.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ajanta Caves” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The rock-cut monasteries of their age at Karle, Bhaja, Nashik and Kanheri, the earliest caves at <b>Ajanta</b>, cut in the same period, and the sculpted stupa at Amaravati, enlarged and decorated in the late Satavahana period, are among the masterpieces of early Indian art, and the Amaravati style…</blockquote><a href="/empire/satavahana-dynasty">Satavahana dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Harishena of the Vatsagulma branch, his minister Varahadeva and other nobles sponsored many of the finest caves at <b>Ajanta</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/vakataka-dynasty">Vakataka dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
